WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
This is the last line of a message an AI is about to receive.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:10.540
"Your action plan must strictly match: sell." A workflow writes that line, to hold a model to a verdict it has already been given.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.060
So who decided the verdict?

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.280
Let me take you to the meeting.

00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:18.693
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni, in for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:23.393
This week I contributed to Mycroft, a book and a working repository of finance pipelines.

00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:24.493
It calls them recipes.

00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:29.893
One of them, the What-If Simulation Agent, asks three different questions about a stock, then hands an AI the answers.

00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:34.973
Three voices, one written record, and one question for this whole video: who had a vote, and how much?

00:00:34.873 --> 00:00:40.773
My pull request, now merged, made the six steps before the AI runnable on test data, with checks.

00:00:41.293 --> 00:00:45.153
Every company you'll see is invented, and none of this is investment advice.

00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:48.247
Today's stock is Ironbridge Power.

00:00:48.487 --> 00:00:48.947
Invented.

00:00:49.107 --> 00:00:50.387
Growth, five percent.

00:00:50.287 --> 00:00:50.687
Sentiment, point three five.

00:00:51.747 --> 00:00:54.027
A price-to-earnings of eighty, and an aggressive investor.

00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:56.727
I'll call the workflow the chair, and the three simulators its voices.

00:00:57.327 --> 00:01:00.147
The chair's first job is to make sure every seat has something to read.

00:01:00.287 --> 00:01:02.207
Ironbridge arrived with everything filled in.

00:01:02.227 --> 00:01:05.567
But if a request arrives with nothing, the chair fills every seat itself.

00:01:05.907 --> 00:01:08.927
A company called Unknown Company, growth fifteen percent, sentiment point five.

00:01:10.227 --> 00:01:11.607
The meeting starts anyway.

00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:14.713
First voice: growth against sentiment.

00:01:14.853 --> 00:01:18.773
It sorts a stock into one of nine boxes, low, medium or high on each.

00:01:19.273 --> 00:01:20.673
Ironbridge is low and low.

00:01:20.893 --> 00:01:23.773
The voice says strong sell, with eighty-two percent confidence.

00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:28.393
Second voice: the same stock, judged for three kinds of investor.

00:01:28.293 --> 00:01:29.853
Conservative says avoid.

00:01:30.193 --> 00:01:31.093
Balanced says avoid.

00:01:30.993 --> 00:01:32.773
Aggressive has the loosest limits.

00:01:32.673 --> 00:01:34.293
Growth at least five percent.

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:35.993
Sentiment at least point three five.

00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:37.693
Price-to-earnings no more than eighty.

00:01:37.593 --> 00:01:39.613
Debt to equity no more than one and a half.

00:01:40.173 --> 00:01:41.653
And Ironbridge sits exactly on all four.

00:01:41.553 --> 00:01:42.493
Four out of four.

00:01:42.393 --> 00:01:43.553
Enter a full position.

00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:47.987
Third voice, and this one asks something different.

00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:51.107
Not what should we do, but how close is it to changing its mind.

00:01:51.547 --> 00:01:55.407
It nudges growth and sentiment up and down, six ways, and watches the verdict.

00:01:55.687 --> 00:01:57.427
Three of the six nudges change it.

00:01:57.547 --> 00:01:59.867
The stability score: point oh five, out of one.

00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:01.887
Now they disagree.

00:02:02.007 --> 00:02:03.187
The first voice says strong sell.

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:05.187
The second says enter a full position.

00:02:05.707 --> 00:02:06.787
Nobody has made a mistake.

00:02:06.827 --> 00:02:08.107
They were asked different questions.

00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:10.887
So the chair weighs them: forty-five, thirty-five and twenty.

00:02:11.107 --> 00:02:12.927
The chair's scale runs from one to five.

00:02:13.087 --> 00:02:15.827
Strong sell sits at the bottom, and enter a full position at the top.

00:02:15.887 --> 00:02:18.827
So a smaller weight on the top can outweigh a bigger weight on the bottom.

00:02:19.127 --> 00:02:22.527
The voices contribute point three seven, one point seven five, and point oh five.

00:02:23.827 --> 00:02:26.007
Together, two point one seven out of five.

00:02:26.387 --> 00:02:29.607
Anywhere from one point eight to two point eight, the chair writes down as sell.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.500
Then the chair writes the minutes.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.080
About four hundred and seventy words.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.640
The stock, each voice, the stress tests, the verdict.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:43.000
And one last instruction, for someone who wasn't in the room: the action plan must strictly match sell.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.700
The AI only ever receives the minutes.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.160
And in the minutes, a number somebody typed looks exactly like a number the chair filled in.

00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:53.633
Here's where I got something wrong.

00:02:53.793 --> 00:02:59.513
To check the original, I wrote a second minute-taker, a Python copy of those six steps, and ran both on the same meetings.

00:02:59.973 --> 00:03:01.733
On two requests, it wrote a different verdict.

00:03:01.953 --> 00:03:06.853
Those were requests where growth was typed as the letters A, B, C, and sentiment was left as empty text.

00:03:07.073 --> 00:03:11.033
Python and JavaScript disagree about the smallest of a list when one entry isn't a number.

00:03:11.173 --> 00:03:11.993
I fixed it.

00:03:12.213 --> 00:03:18.333
Now both write the same minutes, to the letter, on all twenty-three requests, and a test keeps that slip from coming back.

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.753
Nothing was sent anywhere.

00:03:22.113 --> 00:03:27.693
The step that would call the AI, and the step that would save the answer, are written down as hand-offs, marked not approved.

00:03:28.173 --> 00:03:32.173
Five items in the recipe are still open, and it still says draft, on purpose.

00:03:33.333 --> 00:03:36.853
If an AI is going to summarise your next meeting, have you read the minutes?

00:03:37.013 --> 00:03:37.733
Who had a vote?

00:03:37.873 --> 00:03:38.813
And how much of one?

00:03:39.053 --> 00:03:41.313
A good answer names the voices and their weights.

00:03:41.573 --> 00:03:43.613
"The AI will figure it out" doesn't count.

00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:47.047
Adjourned.

00:03:46.947 --> 00:03:50.667
Tanmay Kulkarni, in for Humanitarians AI, signing off.

